= Cumin

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — jīraka is one of the most-cited spice-drugs of the saṃhitās and a foundational dīpana-pācana (digestive-supportive) agent. Greco-Roman: fully classical — kýminon is in Dioscorides, Hippocrates, Pliny, and the biblical Old and New Testaments (the Greek and Hebrew "cumin" texts). Chinese: not classical proper for_ Cuminum cyminum_; the canonical Chinese 茴香 (huíxiāng) is fennel (see fennel entry); cumin entered Chinese pharmacy as a late and minor import. Modern: ubiquitous Indian, Mexican, Middle Eastern, North African spice; the modern Spanish_ comino / Mexican comino _is a Spanish-colonial transmission of the Mediterranean tradition._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Cuminum cyminum_ L., family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae); the drug is the dried ripe *fruit* (the "cumin seed" of commerce; botanically a schizocarp). The closely-related _black cumin / kālā jīrā / Bunium persicum_ is a different species (and the unrelated _Nigella sativa_, the "black seed" of Mediterranean/Middle Eastern tradition, is often confusingly also called "black cumin" but is *not* an Apiaceae). _Cuminum_ from Greek κύμινον (_kýminon_); the further etymology is Semitic — Hebrew _kammon_ (כַּמֹּן), Aramaic _kammōnā_ — and the word is shared across the Mediterranean.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _κύμινον_); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _cuminum_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_, on _κύμινον_ — a Semitic loan, well-attested across the ancient Mediterranean.]

*English:* _Cumin_, from Old French _cumin_, from Latin _cuminum_, from Greek _kýminon_. The word is one of the older spice-vocabulary continuities.

*Sanskrit:* जीरक (_jīraka_), जीर (_jīra_) — from the root √_jṝ_ "to digest, age, become decrepit" — *"the one that digests"*: the Sanskrit name encodes the dīpana-pācana action directly. Synonyms: अजाजी (_ajājī_), उग्रगन्ध (_ugragandha_, "strong-smelling"), दीपक (_dīpaka_, "kindler, digestive"). Three classical cumins are recognized:
- *श्वेत-जीरक (śveta-jīraka)*, "white cumin" — _Cuminum cyminum_, the standard cumin.
- *कृष्ण-जीरक (kṛṣṇa-jīraka)*, "black cumin" — _Bunium persicum_ (kālā jīrā, the Himalayan / Iranian black cumin); a distinct drug.
- *उपकुञ्चिका (upakuñcikā)* / कलोंजी (kalonji) — _Nigella sativa_, the "black seed"; also sometimes called "kṛṣṇa jīraka" creating the standard cumin-confusion.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _jīraka_, _kṛṣṇa-jīraka_, _ugragandha_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, on the three jīrakas. The root-etymology of jīraka from √_jṝ_ is the classical and modern lexicographic consensus.]

*Hindi:* जीरा (_jīrā_) — same.

*Hebrew (biblical):* _kammon_ (כַּמֹּן) — Isaiah 28:25, 27 (the agricultural-method parable: "Does he not... scatter the cumin?"). The Greek New Testament _kýminon_ at Matthew 23:23 (Jesus's reproach of the Pharisees for tithing of "mint, dill, and cumin" — the King James "anise" at Matthew 23:23 is an Old English translation of ánēthon, which is actually dill; modern translations correct to "dill"). The biblical-Hebrew references are the foundational Western-classical attestations of cumin alongside the Greek philological tradition.#footnote[Isaiah 28:25–27 — Hebrew Bible / Masoretic; Septuagint kýminon. Matthew 23:23 — Greek NT _kýminon_. The Anglo-classical "anise" / "dill" translation issue: see _anise.typ_ and _fennel.typ_; modern translations correct to "dill" for ánēthon.]

*Chinese:* 孜然 (zīrán) — the modern transliteration / borrowing for cumin; the Chinese-canonical spice 茴香 (huíxiāng) is *fennel*, a different Apiaceae. Cumin entered Chinese cuisine through Silk Road trade and is regionally established in northwest China (Xinjiang) and Hui-Muslim cuisine; the formal Bencao tradition gives 孜然 a relatively brief entry.

*Modern Spanish / Portuguese / Latin American:* _comino_ / _cominho_ — central spice in Mexican, Tex-Mex, and Brazilian (especially Northeast) cooking.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Cumin is not a classical Bencao Gangmu primary drug. The 孜然 entry exists in regional / Hui-Muslim cuisine / modern Chinese phytomedicine; placement on Chinese axes is reasoned and brief._

By reasoned placement (analogous to fennel and other warming Apiaceae): pungent, warm; Spleen, Stomach, Kidney. Reasoned actions: 散寒理氣 (disperses cold, regulates qi), 健胃 (strengthens stomach), 殺蟲 (anthelminthic — mild). The functional register parallels the Ayurvedic dīpana-pācana.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Jīraka is one of the most-cited spice-drugs in the saṃhitās — a foundational dīpana-pācana ingredient and a near-universal cooking spice in Indian dietetics.#footnote[Jīraka in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; standard nighaṇṭus.]

*Rasa:* Kaṭu (pungent), Tikta (bitter — mild).
*Guṇa:* Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry), Tīkṣṇa (sharp).
*Vīrya:* Uṣṇa (warming).
*Vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent).

*Doṣa:* Pacifies Kapha and Vāta; aggravates Pitta in excess.

*Karma:*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — the central, name-encoded action; kindles agni and supports āma-pācana.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta gas and bloating.
- _Garbhāśaya-balya, stanya-vardhana_ — uterine-strengthening, galactagogue; classical postpartum use.
- _Kāsa-hara, śvāsa-hara_ — relieves cold-pattern cough and dyspnea.
- _Pravāhikā, atisāra_ — useful in mild diarrhea (the carminative-antispasmodic action).
- _Krimi-ghna_ — anthelminthic (modest).
- Used in: agnimāndya (digestive weakness), ādhmāna (gas), ajīrṇa (indigestion), postpartum recovery, cold-pattern respiratory conditions.

The three-jīraka comparison: śveta-jīraka (standard cumin) is the most-used; kṛṣṇa-jīraka (Bunium) is more aromatic and is used for special preparations; upakuñcikā (Nigella) is bitter, distinct.#footnote[Karma compiled from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu and standard Ayurvedic clinical practice. Standard cumin (jīraka) is universal in Indian cooking — _tadkā / chhonk / tarka_ (the seasoning step in Indian cuisine almost always begins with cumin in hot ghee).]

=== Viruddha

- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - Generally very safe; widely used as food.
  - Mild antiplatelet effect at high doses; theoretical interaction with anticoagulants.
  - Apiaceae allergy.
  - Cumin essential oil concentrated — not for unsupervised internal use.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Cumin is canonical in Tibb and a foundational digestive carminative. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* كمون (_kammūn_) — the canonical Tibb name; same in Persian and Urdu (_kammūn / kammon / zīrah_); the Hindi-Urdu *zīrah* (زیرہ) carries forward from Persian _zīrā_, which is itself a relative of the Sanskrit _jīraka_.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* not explicitly Qur'ānic but appears in dietetic hadith and in the Tibb al-Nabawī compendia.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 2nd–3rd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _kammūn_; varies in source — 2nd degree per Avicenna, 3rd per Anṭākī). The Hot-Dry classification converges with the Ayurvedic _uṣṇa vīrya_ and with the Sanskrit-name etymology (_jīraka_ from √_jṝ_ "to digest" — the Sanskrit-and-Arabic readings agree).

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-mi'da* (gastric tonic — the principal action); *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* (carminative, "wind-breaker"); *muḥallil* (resolvent); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (mild diuretic); *mughayyir li-l-balgham* (phlegm-modifying); *muḥaffif li-l-warm* (anti-swelling); *mudirr li-l-laban* (galactagogue — the classical Arabic-Persian-Indian convergent postpartum use); *muqawwī al-bāh* (mild aphrodisiac).

*Istemālāt:* dyspepsia (the foundational indication); flatulence and bloating; cold-pattern abdominal pain; postpartum / lactation support; cold-pattern menstrual cramps; weak urination; cold-pattern dysuria; intestinal worms.

*Mawāne':* bilious / hot-pattern dyspepsia; pregnancy at high dose (the uterine-stimulant tendency); peptic ulcer in inflammatory state.

*Muṣliḥ:* vinegar; lemon; rose water for the heating tendency.

*Badal:* caraway (*karawiyā*) — closely related Apiaceae; fennel for milder action.

*Tibb formulas:* *Sufūf-i Kammūn* — cumin powder for indigestion; *Joshānda-yi Kammūn* — cumin decoction for dyspepsia and postpartum cramps; *Maʿjūn-i Hāḍim* (digestive electuary) — cumin as a core ingredient; *Sharbat-i Bazūrī* — diuretic syrup including cumin among the seeds; *Itrīfal-i Kishnīz* and other digestive compound powders include cumin.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _kammūn_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; al-Rāzī, *al-Ḥāwī*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _zīrah / kammūn_ entry.#footnote[Tibb kammūn: Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār. The tri-system convergence (Ayurvedic-Greco-Roman-Tibb) on digestive-carminative use is one of the cleanest in spice pharmacology.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Ayurveda

- *Jīraka cūrṇa* — simple powder; 1–3 g.
- *Jīrakādi cūrṇa, Hingvāṣṭaka cūrṇa* — compound digestive powders containing jīraka (see asafoetida.typ for Hingvāṣṭaka).
- *Postpartum jeera-pānī / jīraka-water* — jīraka tea taken postpartum as galactagogue and digestive tonic; universal North-Indian postpartum practice.
- *Tadkā / chhonk* — the seasoning preparation in nearly every Indian dish; cumin in hot ghee at the start of cooking is the universal practice.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

- *Dioscorides* (III.59, _kýminon_) treats cumin extensively — heating, drying, useful for stomach, breath, urinary, headache; the classical Greek pharmacy.
- *Pliny* (_N.H._ XIX, XX) discusses cumin.
- *Roman cookery (Apicius)* uses cumin heavily.
- *Biblical references* (above) — Isaiah, Matthew.
- *Medieval European*: continued use; the *quattuor semina calida minora* (anise, caraway, cumin, fennel) — the canonical European carminative quartet (see anise.typ, fennel.typ).
- *Hildegard* mentions cumin.
- *Modern European*: persists in Spanish, French (Provençal), German, and the global cuisines descending from European spice traditions.
- *Mexican / Tex-Mex*: cumin is foundational, brought by Spanish conquest; chili-cumin is the canonical Mexican-American spice combination.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.59 (Beck); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XIX, XX (Loeb); Apicius. Medieval European: pharmacy history. Mexican-cuisine cumin: standard Latin-American culinary history.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Volatile oil (2–5% of dried seed):* dominated by *cuminaldehyde* (the characteristic aroma, ~30–50% of oil), *cymene*, *terpinene*, *β-pinene*, *linalool*.
- *Fixed oil ~10–20%*.
- Flavonoids; phenolic acids.
- Iron, manganese, calcium — substantial in the seed.

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Carminative / antispasmodic*: the cuminaldehyde and terpene fraction; modest clinical evidence in functional dyspepsia and IBS.
- *Antioxidant*.
- *Glycemic*: small clinical evidence for modest hypoglycemic effect in type-2 diabetes.
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro broad-spectrum.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Ayurvedic dīpana-pācana + Dioscoridean digestive + modern carminative pharmacology converge cleanly.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary use*: up to ~3 g/day in cooking is the dietary register; well-tolerated.
- *Ayurvedic medicinal*: ~1–3 g/day powder.
- *Essential oil*: not for unsupervised internal use.
- *Risk groups*: pregnancy at high-essential-oil doses (culinary safe); Apiaceae allergy; high-dose concurrent with anticoagulants.

